  One thing blocks me from just writing it. Here's the current flow:

  [34mfunction[39m [33mlogin[39m(username, password) { ... [34mreturn[39m { [36msuccess[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36muser[39m: username }; }

  and the only caller, the submit handler, reads just [38;5;153m#username[39m and [38;5;153m#password[39m from the form. [1mThere is no userId anywhere
[0m  [1mat the call site to pass in.[0m So "add a userId parameter" has a few materially different readings, and they don't cost
  the same later:

  - [1muserId comes back from login, not into it[0m — the server identifies the user; the form can't know an ID before
    authenticating. Tracking then reads [38;5;153mresult.userId[39m. Costs: no new parameter at all, so it's not literally what you
    asked for. Rules out nothing; this is the cheapest to change later.
  - [1mCaller supplies it from existing context[0m — something outside the form (a tenant, a device, a prior session) already
    holds the ID and threads it in. Costs: I need to know where that value lives, and the submit handler changes too.
    Locks in a parameter order other callers would depend on.
  - [1mAdd the parameter as a stub now[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m, callers pass [38;5;153mnull[39m until a real source exists.
    Costs: an unused parameter in the signature and a dead argument at the one call site. Cheap to add, mildly annoying
    to remove once the real source arrives.

  My recommendation is the first. At the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called, the user is by definition not yet identified — the ID
  is an [3moutput[0m of authentication, not an input to it. Threading in an ID the caller made up would let you "track" a
  value the server never confirmed, which defeats the point of tracking who logged in.
